We want to find the area of the biggest square. The area of this square is composed of the center white square and the four red triangles. Because the inner square has an area of <math>(5-2)</math>, <math>3</math>, squared, <math>9</math>, it also has a length of <math>\sqrt{9}=3</math>. The heights of each of the red triangles are 1 (because the gray squares have lengths of one), and the area of one triangle is namely <math>\frac{3 \cdot 1}{2}</math>. Thus, the combined area of the four triangles is <math>4 \cdot \frac 32=6</math>. Furthermore, the area of the smaller square is <math>9</math>. We add these to see that the area of the large square is <math>9+6=15\implies \boxed{\textbf{(C)}}</math>.

