filldraw((5,5)--(6,5)--(6,6)--(5,6)--cycle,red);
</asy> <math>\newline</math>

The total area of the colored regions is <math>20</math>, so the probability that Sonya lands in a colored region is <math>\frac{20}{36} = \frac{5}{9}</math>. We can calculate the probability that Sonya gets out of each type of region. <math>\newline</math>

Case 1: Sonya chooses a blue region.

The probability that Sonya chooses a blue region is <math>\frac{16}{20} = \frac{4}{5}</math>. One direction can let her out, so the probability that she chooses the right one is <math>\frac{1}{4}</math>. Finally, the probability that Sonya chooses a distance to get her out is <math>\frac{1}{2}</math>. So, the probability that she chooses a blue region and gets out is <math>\frac{5}{9} \cdot \frac{4}{5} \cdot \frac{1}{4} \cdot \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{18}</math>. <math>\newline</math>

Case 2: Sonya choose a red region.

The probability that Sonya chooses a red region is <math>\frac{4}{20} = \frac{1}{5}</math>. Two directions can let her out, so the probability that she chooses one of them is <math>\frac{1}{2}</math>. Finally, the probability that Sonya chooses a distance to get her out is <math>\frac{1}{2}</math>. So, the probability that she chooses a red region and gets out is <math>\frac{5}{9} \cdot \frac{1}{5} \cdot \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{36}</math>. <math>\newline</math>

So, the probability that Sonya gets out is <math>\frac{1}{18} + \frac{1}{36} = \frac{3}{36} = </math> <math>\boxed{\textbf{(B) }\tfrac{1}{12}}.</math>

