Note: You can cheese/fakesolve <imath>c</imath> by assuming <imath>c</imath> is real. But there is a good reason for it, within the parameters of this problem:

<imath>c</imath> lies on a circle of radius 4, centered at the origin. Thus, <imath>{4c-3}</imath> is also a circle, centered at <imath>-3</imath>. Luckily, the center of this adjusted circle is a negative real number, so the most negative point on that circle (<imath>w = -19</imath>) has the largest magnitude (and so its square root also has the largest magnitude of any square root), and its positive square root is the most purely imaginary square root ("vertical"). [We ignore the alternative negative square root, since the problem asks for the most positive value.] 

